Q: Detect dark application style/theme of currently used desktop in Qt A: <p>For those looking for a simple <code>isDarkMode</code> function that works in Qt 6.4 and 6.5:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;QGuiApplication&gt;
#include &lt;QPalette&gt;
#include &lt;QStyleHints&gt;

inline bool isDarkMode() {
#if QT_VERSION &gt;= QT_VERSION_CHECK(6, 5, 0)
  const auto scheme = QGuiApplication::styleHints()-&gt;colorScheme();
  return scheme == Qt::ColorScheme::Dark;
#else
  const QPalette defaultPalette;
  const auto text = defaultPalette.color(QPalette::WindowText);
  const auto window = defaultPalette.color(QPalette::Window);
  return text.lightness() &gt; window.lightness();
#endif // QT_VERSION
}
</code></pre>

Q: Set env variables before running C# integration test in VS Code A: <h2>Command line interface</h2>
<p>This is supported through an option of <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/tools/dotnet-test-vstest#options" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>dotnet test</code></a></p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong><code>-e|--environment &lt;NAME=&quot;VALUE&quot;&gt;</code></strong></p>
<p>Sets the value of an environment variable. Creates the variable if it doesn't exist, overrides if it does exist. Use of this option will force the tests to be run in an isolated process. The option can be specified multiple times to provide multiple variables.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I tried it for the 3 major test frameworks on .NET 9:</p>
<pre><code>namespace msTestFramework;

[TestClass]
public sealed class Test1
{
    [TestMethod]
    public void TestMethod1()
    {
        Assert.AreEqual(&quot;bar&quot;, Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(&quot;foo&quot;));
    }
}

</code></pre>
<pre><code>
namespace nUnitFramework;

public class Tests
{
    [Test]
    public void Test1()
    {
        Assert.That(Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(&quot;foo&quot;), Is.EqualTo(&quot;bar&quot;));
    }
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>
namespace xUnitFramework;

public class UnitTest1
{
    [Fact]
    public void Test1()
    {
        Assert.Equal(&quot;bar&quot;, Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(&quot;foo&quot;));
    }
}

</code></pre>
<p><code>dotnet test</code> -&gt; all test fail</p>
<p><code>dotnet test -e foo=bar</code> -&gt; all tests pass</p>
<h2>Visual Studio Code</h2>
<h3>Terminal</h3>
<p>You can open the <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/terminal/getting-started" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Terminal</a> inside of VS Code and invoke the CLI commands.</p>
<h3>Command Palette via custom Task</h3>
<p>I don't think the C# Dev Kit supports this out of the box.
There are &quot;Test Profiles&quot; (Run, Debug, Coverage) but according to my brief research, these are hard coded and not customizable. It's also unclear what they actually are/do in detail and if they'd be capable of performing such customization.</p>
<p>However, VS Code allows you to define <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/debugtest/tasks#_custom-tasks" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Custom Tasks</a>, with some integration for testing:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>group</strong>: Defines to which group the task belongs. In the example, it belongs to the <code>test</code> group. Tasks that belong to the test group can be executed by running <strong>Run Test Task</strong> from the <strong>Command Palette</strong>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here's what I came up with:</p>
<pre><code>{
    // See https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkId=733558
    // for the documentation about the tasks.json format
    &quot;version&quot;: &quot;2.0.0&quot;,
    &quot;tasks&quot;: [
        {
            &quot;label&quot;: &quot;foo = bar&quot;,
            &quot;type&quot;: &quot;shell&quot;,
            &quot;group&quot;: &quot;test&quot;,
            &quot;command&quot;: &quot;dotnet test -e foo=bar&quot;
        }
    ]
}
</code></pre>
<p>And indeed, when I execute the <strong>Run Test Task</strong> from the command palette, the following dropdown appears:</p>
<p><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/659P6FoB.png" alt="foo = bar in command palette" /></p>
<p>The command is then executed in the Terminal. Integration with VS Code GUI is rather &quot;meh&quot; as it does not update the results in the &quot;Testing&quot; view on the left.</p>
<p><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/iVmpm9Sj.png" alt="Testing not updated" /></p>
<p>You can define <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/reference/variables-reference#_input-variables" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Input variables</a> to prompt for some value if you don't want to hard code it, e.g.:</p>
<pre><code>
{
    // See https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkId=733558
    // for the documentation about the tasks.json format
    &quot;version&quot;: &quot;2.0.0&quot;,
    &quot;tasks&quot;: [
        {
            &quot;label&quot;: &quot;Test with custom foo value&quot;,
            &quot;type&quot;: &quot;shell&quot;,
            &quot;group&quot;: &quot;test&quot;,
            &quot;command&quot;: &quot;dotnet test -e foo=${input:fooValueID}&quot;
        }
    ],
    &quot;inputs&quot;: [
        {
        &quot;id&quot;: &quot;fooValueID&quot;,
        &quot;type&quot;: &quot;promptString&quot;,
        &quot;description&quot;: &quot;value for 'foo' environment variable&quot;,
        &quot;default&quot;: &quot;&quot;
        }
    ]
}
</code></pre>
<p>UX is like this then:</p>
<p><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/EDYkJ3IZ.png" alt="value prompt" /></p>
<p>Note that you could achieve the same by specify the environment variable at the VS Code task level like so:</p>
<pre><code>
{
    // See https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkId=733558
    // for the documentation about the tasks.json format
    &quot;version&quot;: &quot;2.0.0&quot;,
    &quot;tasks&quot;: [
        {
            &quot;label&quot;: &quot;Test with custom foo value&quot;,
            &quot;type&quot;: &quot;shell&quot;,
            &quot;group&quot;: &quot;test&quot;,
            &quot;command&quot;: &quot;dotnet test&quot;,
            &quot;options&quot;: 
            {
                &quot;env&quot;: 
                {
                    &quot;foo&quot;: &quot;${input:fooValueID}&quot;
                }
            }
        }
    ],
    &quot;inputs&quot;: [
        {
        &quot;id&quot;: &quot;fooValueID&quot;,
        &quot;type&quot;: &quot;promptString&quot;,
        &quot;description&quot;: &quot;value for 'foo' environment variable&quot;,
        &quot;default&quot;: &quot;&quot;
        }
    ]
}
</code></pre>
<p>This is however limited to environment variables specifically and does not give access to the rich arguments of <code>dotnet test</code>, which you'd likely want to expose to the command palette, e.g. to do <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/testing/unit-testing-mstest-writing-tests-organizing#test-categorization" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Test categorization</a> and filter out <code>[TestCategory(&quot;Slow&quot;)]</code> during local development work.</p>

Q: Laravel Livewire 3 | get current route in component A: <p>You can save your current route's name into poblic property when mounting the Livewire component. Thus the route's name will be preserved even during subsequent updating requests.</p>
<p><code>#[Locked]</code> attribute prevents users from manipulating the value.</p>
<pre><code>use Livewire\Attributes\Locked;
use Livewire\Component;

class Test extends Component
{

  #[Locked]
  public string $currentRoute;

  public function mount() {
    $this-&gt;currentRoute = request()-&gt;route()-&gt;getName();
  }

}
</code></pre>

Q: passing date as a parameter in stored procedure (oracle) A: <p>One possible solution is to use a java.sql.timestamp instead of java.sql.Date.  Also you can refer to <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/cd/B19306_01/java.102/b14188/datamap.htm#CHDBJAGH" rel="nofollow noreferrer">SQL and PL/SQL Mappings to Oracle and JDBC Types</a> in order to verify the correct data type mapping.</p>

<p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/22856931/converting-java-date-to-sql-timestamp#22857114">This is an example of how get java.sql.timestamp</a></p>

<pre><code>java.util.Date utilDate = new java.util.Date();
Calendar cal = Calendar.getInstance();
cal.setTime(utilDate);
cal.set(Calendar.MILLISECOND, 0);
System.out.println(new java.sql.Timestamp(utilDate.getTime()));
System.out.println(new java.sql.Timestamp(cal.getTimeInMillis()));
</code></pre>
